  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 958 AM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nToday will see the best (worst?) of both worlds as very hot\ntemperatures expand across the RGV/eastward and monsoonal moisture\nsets up out west. The upper high sits over E NM today, allowing\nlowland temps to climb to about 105 and warranting Heat\nAdvisories. Moisture rotates around the western periphery of the\nhigh, bringing isolated to scattered storm chances out west this\nafternoon and evening. Slow storm motions to the northwest and \nPWs around 1.2\" will result in flash flood potential west of the \nDivide. HREF max 6-hr rainfall shows up to 2\" of rain with \nconsistent bulls-eyes in the NM Bootheel this evening. Somewhat \nlower amounts are forecast in the Gila this afternoon before \nfocusing further south. With saturated soils from recent rainfall\nand this heavy rain potential, Flood Watches have been posted.\nMainly dry conditions are expected from Deming eastward.\n\nThe upper high doesn't move much through Thu, flattening out a bit\ndue to a shortwave passing through the Central Rockies. Moisture\nlevels decrease somewhat out west through midweek with lower storm\nchances than today. Temps stay above normal through the workweek\nwith more Heat Advisories likely, especially on Fri as the high\nstretches west. \n\nHeading into the weekend, the high parks itself over AZ, allowing\nmoisture and shortwaves to rotate in from the north. Scattered \nmtn storms are modeled each afternoon, drifting to the south and \nwest with more isolated coverage in the lowlands. Gusty outflows\nand blowing dust are possible for the lowlands. Better moisture \nand storm chances will slightly lower highs towards 100 for the \nlowlands.",
